<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mumbai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bombay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mumbai, formerly Bombay, is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built on a group of islands on the west coast. Portugal ceded the islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1661 as part of the dowry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> made them its main western base. As capital of the Bombay Presidency it grew into a great port and industrial city, and it was officially renamed Mumbai in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mumbai.jpg" alt="Mumbai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rutiknatekar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_Bandra-Worli_Sea_Link.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bombay</b>’s cotton mills and the Tata steelworks at Jamshedpur, opened in 1912, showed the growth of Indian industry, but overall incomes grew slowly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Indian cinema, with Hindi films from <b>Mumbai</b> and large industries in Tamil, Telugu and other languages, produces more films than any other country, and a diaspora of tens of millions carries Indian culture and influence abroad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The pearls were sold through merchants to markets in <b>Bombay</b> and beyond, and the trade linked Sharjah to India, Persia and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet Rajputana was also the homeland of the Marwari merchant communities, whose firms, such as those of the Birla and Bajaj families, had become leading industrial houses in Calcutta, <b>Bombay</b> and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 17th century many Parsis moved to <b>Bombay</b>, where under British rule they became leading merchants, shipbuilders, industrialists and philanthropists.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>About three-fifths of the subcontinent formed the provinces of British India, such as Bengal, <b>Bombay</b> and Madras, governed by a viceroy and a small corps of British officials, the Indian Civil Service.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
